From: skyjet18 <57456827+skyjet18@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 11:16:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 50/50] hls: added support for #EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY

Ported to FFmpeg 9.0.2 from skyjet18/FFmpeg release/6.1-patched 350749ce97
(subject typo "hlc" fixed).

This changes HLS for every libavformat user: at a discontinuity the
segment demuxer is restarted and the timestamps of the new region are
shifted so that the stream stays continuous (an ad stitched into a
programme no longer makes the timestamps jump), and a playlist that is
switched in during playback is positioned by timestamp instead of by
media sequence number.

Port: read_data_continuous() of 9.0.2 is structured differently
(reload_playlist(), input_read_done cleared on entry, byte-range
connection reuse with input_reuse/segment_reusable()). The region check
sits where a segment is opened and after the advance to the next
segment, both compare the discontinuity sequence. discontinuity_restart()
closes input and input_next: with http_persistent (the default) the kept
connection would otherwise be read on as the new segment, since the top
of the function clears input_read_done, and the first segment of the
region would be skipped. segment_reusable() does not join a byte range
across a discontinuity.
Review: the original continued a region at first_timestamp plus the
durations from the first segment of the playlist, which is wrong for a
live window: the first packet is live_start_index segments behind the
window start and the window moves, so at the first ad break the
timestamps would have jumped by the time played so far. Each playlist now
anchors its timeline on the segment of its first packet (timeline_seq_no,
timeline_time) and parse_playlist() moves the anchor along as segments
expire; find_timestamp_in_playlist() counts from the anchor, so the
timestamp selection of select_cur_seq_no() holds for live playlists too,
and first_timestamp of a live playlist counts back to the window start as
it does for EVENT playlists (9.0.2 already moves it along on reloads),
from the first segment of the region rather than cur_seq_no, which small
segments move on before the first packet comes out.
The offsets of the regions are kept per discontinuity sequence in
HLSContext, shared by all playlists: renditions keep their relative
timing, and a seek back into a known region or a playlist switched in
later reuses the offset instead of estimating it again from the rounded
EXTINF durations (the original did that after every seek, also in
playlists without any discontinuity). Subtitle playlists (9.0.2 reads
them with a webvtt demuxer per segment) take the offset of their region
from the audio/video playlists, their first cue does not mark the segment
start. Without #EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY-SEQUENCE the numbering of a reloaded
playlist is continued from the previous list, else every discontinuity
leaving the window would restart the demuxer again. hls_read_seek() and
recheck_discard_flags() set the region of the segment they land on and
drop a pending restart; current_segment() returns NULL for a sequence
number before the window instead of indexing before the array. A
misplaced or invalid #EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY-SEQUENCE is ignored with a
warning instead of rejecting the playlist. 0046: EVENT playlists already
select by timestamp, the new live branch sits behind it. 0048: after the
resync to the live edge the next segment open compares the discontinuity
sequence and restarts the demuxer if the region changed. FATE tests
fate-hls-discontinuity and fate-hls-discontinuity-seek as in the
original, in the FATE_HLSENC_LAVFI list of 9.0.2 (the FATE_HLSENC list
requires SAMPLES there, the tests need none).
